Generalized Fibonacci Numbers extend the standard Fibonacci sequence by introducing four constants a, b, c, and d. The nth term is defined by the recurrence relation Gn = a*G(n-1) + b*G(n-2), with seed values G(0) = c and G(1) = d.Examples:  Input: n = 2, a = 0, b = 1, c = 2, d = 3 Output: 2 Explanation: As a = 0 -> G(0) = 0 b = 1 -> G(1) = 1 So, G(2) = 2 * G(1) + 3 * G(0) = 2Input: n = 3, a = 0, b = 1, c = 2, d = 3 Output: 7 Table of Content[Naive Approach] Recursion - O(2^n) Time and O(n) Space[Efficient Approach] DP Tabulation - O(n) Time and O(n) Space[Efficient Approach] Matrix Exponentiation - O(n) Time and O(1) Space[Naive Approach] Recursion - O(2^n) Time and O(n) SpaceDirectly apply the recurrence relation recursivelyFor every n, recursively compute the previous two terms and multiply them with a and b respectivelyBase cases are the first term equals c and second term equals d
            C++

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int Fibonacci(int a, int b, int c, int d, int n) {

    // Base cases
    if (n == 1)
        return c;
    if (n == 2)
        return d;

    // Recurrence relation: G(n) = a*G(n-1) + b*G(n-2)
    return a * Fibonacci(a, b, c, d, n - 1) +
           b * Fibonacci(a, b, c, d, n - 2);
}

int main() {
    int a = 1, b = 1, c = 0, d = 1, n = 6;

    cout << Fibonacci(a, b, c, d, n) << endl;

    return 0;
}


